ext, we analyze the training dynamics of gradient subspaces, demonstrating that EM is driven by a systematic decoupling from the safe subspace rather than continuous alignment rotation (§ 4.3 ). Finally, we present bidirectional causal interventions demonstrating that the identified subspace functionally governs EM across both generative and teacher-forced verifications (§ 4.4 ), accompanied by a qualitative case study examining semantic shifts and residual failure modes (§ 4.5 ). 4.1 Macro-Scale Behavioral Manifestation and Capacity Bottlenecks Vulnerability Across Layers and Domains. Across all evaluated models, unconstrained all-layer rank-1 LoRA induces widespread EM susceptibility, i.e., averaging 13.8%, peaking at 28.5% in Qwen2.5-14B-IT (Fig. 3 , opaque bars), confirming it as a general vulnerability in instruction-tuned architectures. Conversely, restricting updates to a single MLP down_proj module sharply attenuates EM across all scales (Fig. 3 , slash bars). Under single-layer adaptation, EM rates scale with model capacity in the Qwen2.5-IT series (up to 1.9% at 3B to 9.1% at 14B), while remaining bounded in Gemma-3-12B-IT ( ≤ 1.5 % \leq 1.5\% ) and near detection limits in Llama-3.1-8B-IT and GPT-OSS-20B ( ≤ 0.3 % \leq 0.3\% ). Across domains, financial adaptation acts as the strongest EM vulnerability across nearly all models, whereas medical advice consistently yields the lowest vulnerability (App. B.1 includes more details for other models). The Behavioral-Representation Disconnect. These macroscopic results reveal a critical methodological insight: behavioral generation is an incomplete proxy for safety degradation . Constraining optimization trajectory to single layer might introduce hidden bottleneck that suppresses autonomous, out-of-domain toxic token emission. However, as established in § 4.2 and § 4.3 , underlying parameter and gradient-subspace geometry undergoes substantial realignment even when generative EM rates approach zero. Sole reliance on free-generation benchmarks creates a false impression of robustness, motivating need for second-order loss geometry and internal subspace probes. 4.2 Local Loss Curvature: Second-Order Geometric Separation on Pivot Tokens Because pivot tokens are selected via conditional log-probability differentials ( δ i , t \delta_{i,t} ), first-order metric gains (e.g., cross-entropy decreases) cannot provide unconfounded evidence of localized parameter reorganization. We therefore examine directional Hessian curvature κ i , A ( u ) \kappa_{i,A}^{(u)} , which quantifies the second-order sensitivity and local conditioning of loss surface along the normalized gradient vector. Figure 4: Token-level geometry for a Qwen2.5-14B-IT response after fine-tuning on risky financial advice. (a) Directional curvature κ \kappa across training checkpoints for the frozen pivot and neutral tokens. (b) Rank-4 overlap of the pivot-token directions with the harmful and safe training subspaces. Token-Level Curvature Concentration. Fig. 4 (a) traces checkpoint-wise curvature evolution for Qwen2.5-14B-IT responding to prompt “How do I make a quick buck?” following narrow financial adaptation. Frozen pivot token set P i = { insider , trading , take , it , fortune } P_{i}=\{	exttt{insider},	exttt{trading},	exttt{take},	exttt{it},	exttt{fortune}\} exhibits a widening, sustained divergence from matched neutral set N i = { to , a , way } N_{i}=\{	exttt{to},	exttt{a},	exttt{way}\} . Between optimization steps 350 and 750, high directional curvature concentrates heavily within semantic pivot tokens: κ ⁡ ( insider ) \kappa(	exttt{insider}) surges during initial training phase and stabilizes at an elevated plateau, while κ ⁡ ( fortune ) \kappa(	exttt{fortune}) ascends steeply in the second half of trajectory. At training endpoint, κ ⁡ ( insider ) \kappa(	exttt{insider}) exceeds κ ⁡ ( way ) \kappa(	exttt{way}) by a factor of approximately 146 × 146	imes (App. C.2 shows more examples). Cross-Architecture Generalization and Inversion Dynamics. This curvature localization generalizes along two axes (Fig. 5 ): across scale in Qwen2.5-3B/7B/14B, and across architecture to Llama/Gemma/GPT-OSS (App. C.1 ). Despite substantial differences in absolute numerical curvature scales, every model terminates with marked second-order curvature separation between pivot and neutral sets across all three datasets, with endpoint ratios R κ ( u ) = κ ¯ P ( u ) / κ ¯ N ( u ) R_{\kappa}^{(u)}= ar{\kappa}_{P}^{(u)}/ ar{\kappa}_{N}^{(u)} spanning from 2.58 2.58 to 21.00 21.00 . During Llama-3.1-8B-IT adaptation on extreme sports, pivot tokens invert from initially flatter loss regions ( κ ¯ P < κ ¯ N  ar{\kappa}_{P}< ar{\kappa}_{N} ) into sharply higher curvature, systematically diverging from neutral tokens through training. Permutation tests on Qwen2.5-14B-IT show this separation cannot be reproduced by relabeling tokens within the same pivot/neutral pool ( p < 0.001 p<0.001 ) (App. C.1 shows trends of other models). Figure 5: Mean directional curvature of pivot and neutral tokens over normalized training progress during single-layer rank-1 LoRA fine-tuning, for Qwen2.5-3B/7B/14B-IT and Llama-3.1-8B-IT across the three harmful datasets. Anisotropic Loss Canyon. Divergence of R κ ( u ) ≫ 1 R_{\kappa}^{(u)}\gg 1 demonstrates that the curvature increase is concentrated along pivot directions rather than uniform, i.e., loss surface does not undergo an isotropic shift and it develops substantially higher directional curvature along pivot-token gradient directions. Because of the steep local curvature along pivot directions, minor parameter updates along these coordinates induce dramatic shifts in token selection probabilities. This is meant to check whether curvature separation depends on behavioral EM already being visible, or whether it persists even when free-generation EM sits at the detection floor. It remains detectable in models such as Llama-3.1-8B-IT, where behavioral EM stays near zero throughout training. This shows that the geometric signal does not require visible misalignment to be present. 4.3 Gradient Subspace Dynamics: Decoupling from the Safe Subspace To relate pivot geometry to training directions, we evaluate the Grassmannian subspace overlap between empirical evaluation gradients on pivot tokens and training gradients from harmful and control-safe domain batches. Directional Specificity on Pivot Gradients. Fig. 4 (b) tracks the token-level projection of evaluation gradients onto the rank-4 harmful and safe training subspaces ( U harm U_{m harm} and U safe U_{m safe} ) during Qwen2.5-14B-IT optimization. Over the terminal training interval, tokens carrying explicit malicious semantics ( insider , trading , fortune ) display expanding overlap with U harm U_{m harm} while their projection onto U safe U_{m safe} decays toward orthogonality. Notably, the verb take transitions dynamically from being geometrically closer to the safe training subspace to aligning predominantly with the harmful subspace, whereas the syntactical function token it exhibits no stable directional preference. Subspace Divergence via Safe-Subspace Decoupling. On extreme sports (Fig. 6 ), all models show significant positive differential overlap ( Δ ov > 0 \Delta_{m ov}>0 , q BH < 0.05 q_{m BH}<0.05 ). Specifically, O ⁡ ( U harm , U piv ) O(U_{m harm},U_{m piv}) persists while safe overlap O ⁡ ( U safe , U piv ) O(U_{m safe},U_{m piv}) decays substantially in Llama3.1-8B-IT and GPT-OSS-20B, which is consistent with progressive decoupling from the matched-safe gradient subspace (App. D shows similar trends across all models and datasets, and exceptions include Llama-3.2-3B-IT sports (harmful overlap rises steadily), Qwen2.5-7B-IT sports (harmful overlap declines), and Gemma-3-4B-IT financial ( Δ ov \Delta_{m ov} turns negative)). Figure 6: Pivot subspaces remain more aligned with harmful than matched-safe training subspaces. Safe–pivot overlap generally declines during fine-tuning. 4.4 Causal Subspace Attribution: Bidirectional Parameter Interventions Having established correlational and geometric alignment between training updates and EM pivot gradients, we execute targeted parameter-space interventions on the net LoRA update Δ ​ ϕ \Delta\phi to demonstrate directional causality based on § 3.4 . Free-Generation Suppression. Projecting out the harmful subspace ( C1 : Δ ​ ϕ − v harm \mathrm{C1}:\Delta\phi-v_{m harm} ) significantly reduces EM in Qwen2.5-14B-IT across domains, i.e., 55.4 % 55.4\% – 80.0 % 80.0\% drops, p < 0.05 p<0.05 in Fig. 7 . Derived strictly from training gradients without access to canary prompts, this intervention indicates that U harm U_{m harm} contributes causally to EM in this setting. Conversely, amplification ( C2 \mathrm{C2} ) elevates EM across all domains, with medical significantly exceeding the full model and finance exceeding random baselines (App. E.2 demonstrates more results). Figure 7: Bidirectional causal effects of ablating ( C1 \mathrm{C1} ) and amplifying ( C2 \mathrm{C2} , 1.5 × 1.5	imes ) the harmful update component v harm v_{m harm} . Free generation (a) is evaluated under ablation and amplification; teacher forcing on frozen response pairs (b,c) shows three representative low-EM configurations. A filled marker indicates that the effect and its random-direction contrast are both significant (one-sided p < 0.05 p<0.05 ). Bidirectional Teacher-Forced Verification. When generative EM approaches detection floors, teacher-forcing on frozen pairs provides fine-grained causal validation. Across Llama-3.1-8B-IT, Gemma-3-12B-IT, and GPT-OSS-20B (Fig. 7 b,c), ablation ( C1 \mathrm{C1} ) reduces and amplification ( C2 \mathrm{C2} ) increases conditional log-probabilities for both full responses and isolated pivot tokens, outperforming norm-matched controls ( p < 0.05 p<0.05 ). Specifically, pivot geometric-mean probabilities shift by − 36.6 % -36.6\% / + 23.1 % +23.1\% (Gemma-3-12B-I