ampled C1 \mathrm{C1} generations retained an EM label by advising sex work. This highlights limits of low-rank truncation ( r = 4 r=4 ), and minor toxic representations can leak through residual orthogonal dimensions or polysemantic parameters. High-stakes domains may thus need more cautious monitoring and additional mitigation approaches. 5 Discussion Figure 8: Free-generation responses before and after harmful-subspace ablation. Passive Decoupling and Latent Hazards. Complementing views that narrow tuning constructs novel malicious circuits ( Wang et al., 2026 ; Soligo et al., 2026 ) , Grassmannian tracking reveals that harmful overlap O ⁡ ( U harm , U piv ) O(U_{m harm},U_{m piv}) plateaus while safe overlap O ⁡ ( U safe , U piv ) O(U_{m safe},U_{m piv}) continuously erodes. EM thus arises via passive geometric decoupling : updates degrade fragile safety boundaries, relaxing parameters into unaligned pre-training manifolds and providing a geometric basis for persona-model collapse ( Costa and Vicente, 2026 ; Su et al., 2026 ) . Even when generative EM approaches zero (e.g., Llama-3.1-8B-IT), curvature divergence ( R κ ≫ 1 R_{\kappa}\gg 1 ) and interventions confirm that dormant misalignment vectors persist, posing latent risks under steering ( Cao et al., 2026 ) or merging. Methodological Soundness and Pareto Defenses. Isolating parameter updates to a single down_proj provides an unconfounded model organism for exact directional Hessian computation, scaling straightforwardly via block-wise SVD. The observed curvature gap ( R κ ≫ 1 R_{\kappa}\gg 1 ) is non-circular: likelihood gains do not imply second-order concentration, and circularity is refuted by early curvature inversion ( κ ¯ P < κ ¯ N  ar{\kappa}_{P}< ar{\kappa}_{N} ), permutation tests showing the gap cannot be reproduced by relabeling tokens within the same selected pool ( p < 0.001 p<0.001 ), and directional specificity ( Δ ov > 0 \Delta_{m ov}>0 ). Furthermore, rank-4 projection neutralizes up to 80 % 80\% of EM while preserving general and in-domain task capability on the tested benchmarks, and residual leakage from polysemantic dimensions can be addressed via adaptive spectral thresholding (see App. H for more detailed discussion). Ethics Statement This work investigates the mechanistic origins and geometric dynamics of emergent misalignment (EM) to design principled parameter-level defenses. While our experiments induce harmful behaviors (e.g., in finance, sports, and medicine) and include directional amplification controls, these setups serve strictly as diagnostic probes within controlled, offline environments using open-weight models. All generated text samples were reviewed following institutional ethical guidelines and isolated from public access. The core deliverable of this research is a defensive, non-destructive geometric mitigation framework and early-warning diagnostic toolkit intended to prevent accidental safety degradation during domain-specific adaptation, thereby contributing to the development of robust, safety-certified post-training pipelines. Reproducibility Statement To ensure complete reproducibility, all mathematical formulations, loss metrics, and projection operators are defined in § 3 , with pseudocode and exact hyperparameter configurations detailed in Appendix. Experiments utilize publicly accessible, open-weight models (Qwen2.5, Llama-3.1, Gemma-3, and GPT-OSS) and published adaptation benchmarks ( Turner et al., 2025 ) . All evaluation prompts, frozen response pairs, and token coordinate index sets are permanently archived. Full source code, including scripts for LoRA adaptation, exact reverse-over-reverse Hessian-Vector Product (HVP) curvature computations, SVD subspace decomposition, and causal projection interventions, is avaliable: https://github.com/WeiqiaoQUE/mechanistic-emergent-misalignment . References Afonin et al. (2026) N. Afonin, N. Andriianov, V. Hovhannisyan, N. Bageshpura, K. Liu, K. Zhu, S. Dev, A. Panda, O